Earlier this week, <a href="https://daringfireball.net/2026/09/spitballing_predictions_for_apples_october"><em>Daring Fireball</em>'s John Gruber floated Tuesday, October 13</a> as a potential date for Apple to hand out iPhone Duo review units to selected reviewers in person, and it appears that he might have been on to something.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/Apple-October-13.jpeg" alt="" width="1672" height="941" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1067922" /><br/>
<em>Bloomberg</em>'s Mark Gurman today reported that Apple plans to unveil its all-new smart home hub, a second-generation HomePod mini, and a new Apple TV 4K model <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/30/apple-home-products-coming-on-october-13/">on October 13</a>, the same date that Gruber mentioned in his speculative piece a few days ago.<br/>
<br/>
In a <a href="https://daringfireball.net/linked/2026/09/29/fu-october-spitball">separate post</a>, Gruber opined that there is "no way" that Apple would unveil the home hub through an Apple Newsroom announcement only, given it is an all-new device with an all-new interface. "If this is coming, it has to be unveiled with a keynote video and some sort of media event with hands-on demos," he said.<br/>
<br/>
If Gruber is right that the home hub "has to be unveiled with a keynote video," that would set the stage for an online-only Apple Event that would be streamed on Apple's website and YouTube on October 13. Afterwards, Apple would likely hold a hands-on experience for the media, allowing them to demo the products that were announced.<br/>
<br/>
The same thing happened in October 2023. On October 24 that year, Apple <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2023/10/24/apple-october-mac-event-2023/">announced</a> that it would be holding an online-only Apple Event on October 30, which was streamed on Apple's website and YouTube. Later that day, Apple allowed selected journalists and content creators to <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2024/10/30/m4-mac-mini-hands-on-photos/">go hands-on with the Macs</a> unveiled during the event in person.<br/>
<br/>
During the hands-on experience, selected individuals could also receive an iPhone Duo review unit in hand, allowing Apple to avoid a first-of-its-kind iPhone getting lost in the mail before it launches on Friday, October 23. Apple typically gives out review units around a week before a device launches, so the timing would line up.<br/>
<br/>
In any case, mark your calendars, as it sounds like October 13 will be a big day for Apple.<br/>
